---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { getExperience, includedExperiences, experienceFrame } from '../../../data/experiences';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/paths';
const caseIII = getExperience('case-iii');
export function getStaticPaths() { return includedExperiences.some(e => e.slug === 'case-iii') ? [{ params: { trainer: 'case-iii' } }] : []; }
const trainerHref = sitePath(experienceFrame(caseIII));
const contents = [
 ['overview', 'Purpose and learning objectives'], ['sequence', 'Recovery sequence'],
 ['clearance', 'Arrival and marshal clearance'], ['geometry', 'Bearings and the landing area'],
 ['tacan', 'Navigate to the fix'], ['holding', 'Marshal and timing'],
 ['penetration', 'Commence and platform'], ['final', 'Final approach and landing'],
 ['radio', 'Communications'], ['outcomes', 'Bolter and waveoff'],
 ['check', 'Check your understanding'], ['launch', 'Practice: Tomcat exercise'],
 ['sources', 'Sources and applicability'],
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Case III Recoveries" activeSection="manual" activeSubject="naval-operations"
 compactSubjectNav={true}
 breadcrumbs={[{label:'Manual',href:sitePath('manual/')},{label:'Naval Operations',href:sitePath('manual/naval-operations/')},{label:'Case III Recoveries'}]}>
 <section class="status-panel">
  <h2>Drafted — not technically validated</h2>
  <p>Module revision 1 · Conventional fixed-wing, arrested carrier recoveries. Aircraft-agnostic concepts, illustrated using historical US Navy training material. Helicopter and vertical-landing procedures are outside this module.</p>
  <p>Read the recovery sequence, explore the navigation geometry, then practise selected skills in the optional standalone Tomcat exercise.</p>
 </section>
 <nav class="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title"><h2 id="contents-title">In this module</h2>
  <ol>{contents.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
 </nav>
 <h2 id="overview">Purpose and learning objectives</h2>
 <p>Case III provides an instrument recovery to the carrier at night and in weather requiring instrument procedures. It connects a sequenced marshal hold to an instrument approach and the final visual landing task. The Air Boss determines the recovery case. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-3–2-4, 2-22–2-25]</a></p>
 <p>By the end, you should be able to explain the recovery phases; distinguish a radial, inbound course, base recovery course and final bearing; interpret a marshal clearance; explain why timing and configuration matter; and distinguish a landing, bolter and waveoff.</p>
 <p class="note">This is a conceptual reference, not an aircraft checklist. Exact speeds, configuration, approach aids, minima and missed-approach instructions depend on the aircraft, equipment, applicable procedure and clearance. The source is a T-45C training publication: its aircraft actions are not universal.</p>
 <h2 id="sequence">Recovery sequence</h2>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Recovery sequence" tabindex="0"><table>
 <caption>Conceptual sequence based on [1], §204. Read the phase explanations before the exercise.</caption>
 <thead><tr><th scope="col">Phase</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Understand before continuing</th></tr></thead>
 <tbody>
 <tr><th scope="row">Arrival</th><td>Receive the recovery plan.</td><td>Clearance, weather, fuel and available approach.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Marshal</th><td>Separate and sequence arrivals.</td><td>Fix, altitude, holding pattern and expected approach time.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Commence</th><td>Leave marshal in the assigned sequence.</td><td>Time, inbound course and descent profile.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Penetration</th><td>Descend toward the instrument final.</td><td>Platform, level-off and correction to final bearing.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Final</th><td>Follow approach guidance, then transition to landing cues.</td><td>Configuration, guidance validity and landing signal officer calls.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Recovery outcome</th><td>Arrest or safely re-enter the recovery.</td><td>Applicable bolter/waveoff procedure and controller instructions.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <h2 id="clearance">Arrival and marshal clearance</h2>
 <p>Marshal supplies weather and altimeter, recovery case, holding instructions, expected approach time (EAT), expected final bearing, approach frequency and relevant divert/fuel information. Read the clearance as a connected plan: <em>where</em> to wait, <em>at what altitude</em>, <em>when</em> to leave, and <em>which approach</em> follows. Report established in holding and report commencing the approach. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-26–2-27]</a></p>
 <p>Fuel planning remains active throughout the recovery. A delayed approach consumes fuel; a bolter or waveoff adds another circuit. The module does not prescribe a common fuel ladder: the source explicitly makes bingo fuel dependent on aircraft type. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. A-1]</a></p>
 <h2 id="geometry">Bearings and the landing area</h2>
 <dl>
 <dt><strong>Radial</strong></dt><dd>A bearing <em>from</em> the navigation station. Radial plus range locates a fix; it is not a heading from your present position to that fix.</dd>
 <dt><strong>Inbound course</strong></dt><dd>The reciprocal direction toward the station along a radial. The reciprocal of 220° is 040°.</dd>
 <dt><strong>Base recovery course (BRC)</strong></dt><dd>The carrier's magnetic heading for recovery.</dd>
 <dt><strong>Final bearing</strong></dt><dd>The magnetic direction of the landing-area centreline. The angled landing area means this differs from BRC.</dd>
 </dl>
 <p>The first two definitions describe the geometry used below; BRC and final bearing are defined in <a href="#source-p816">[1, glossary pp. A-1, A-3]</a>. Do not substitute BRC for final bearing or assume the marshal radial is always reciprocal to either. The published CV-1 example explicitly provides for correction from the marshal radial to final bearing. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-23]</a></p>
 <h2 id="tacan">Navigate to the fix</h2>
 <p>TACAN (tactical air navigation) provides bearing and DME (distance measuring equipment) range. Position yourself and the fix on a station-centred plot, then find the direction from your position to the fix. Flying the assigned radial as a heading does not solve this point-to-point problem.</p>
 <p>In this educational calculation, bearings are magnetic, north is up and range is in nautical miles (NM). We use a flat plane, horizontal range and no wind; actual DME is slant range. Heading equals track only under the no-wind assumption. The fix is constrained to lie opposite BRC for this exercise; actual clearance geometry can differ.</p>
  <section class="lesson" data-lesson="navigation" aria-labelledby="nav-title">
    <h3 id="nav-title">Practise: heading to the marshal fix</h3>
    <p>Set the ship’s BRC and your own position, estimate the heading to the fix, then check it. The fix is on the BRC + 180° radial for this geometry exercise.</p>
    <div class="lesson-grid">
      <div class="controls">
        <label>BRC (°)<input name="brc" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="0" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <p class="derived">Marshal radial <strong data-fix-radial>180</strong>°</p>
        <label>Fix DME (NM)<input name="fixDme" type="number" min="1" max="60" step="1" value="21" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <label>Your radial (°)<input name="youRadial" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="90" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <label>Your DME (NM)<input name="youDme" type="number" min="0" max="80" step="1" value="30" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <label>Your heading estimate (°)<input name="guess" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <div class="buttons">
          <button type="button" data-check>Check estimate</button>
          <button type="button" data-reveal>Reveal</button>
          <button type="button" data-new>New problem</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 320 320" role="img" aria-label="Compass plot centred on Mother"></svg>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p data-result aria-live="polite">Worked example: from the 090 radial at 30 DME to the 180 radial at 21 DME, the direct track is about 235° for 36.6 NM.</p>
    <noscript><p>The interactive plot needs JavaScript. The worked example above shows the same calculation.</p></noscript>
  </section>
  <details>
    <summary>How the check is calculated</summary>
    <p>Place Mother at the origin with x east and y north. A position on radial <var>r</var> at <var>d</var> NM is x = <var>d</var>·sin <var>r</var>, y = <var>d</var>·cos <var>r</var>. The track from you to the fix is the direction of the difference vector, atan2(Δx, Δy), and the distance is its length. In the worked example you are at (30, 0) and the fix at (0, −21), so Δx = −30, Δy = −21: the track is 180° + atan(30/21) ≈ 235.0° and the distance √1341 ≈ 36.6 NM. With no wind, that track is also the heading. The time shown uses a fixed educational map rate of 250 NM/hour, which is not a groundspeed model.</p>
  </details>


 <h2 id="holding">Marshal and timing</h2>
 <p>Marshal is a sequencing and separation system, not just an orbit near the ship. The assigned radial, range and altitude define your place in it. Plan to arrive at the fix ready to commence at EAT. Reaching the right position at the wrong time loses the intended sequence. The source describes managing the hold to reach the fix on time and reporting a time discrepancy. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-19–2-20]</a></p>
 <p>The historical pattern in [1] is left-hand and six minutes: two two-minute turns plus two one-minute legs. Holding speed is tied to fuel conservation or the aircraft’s specified holding speed. These are the source's documented parameters, not a universal four-minute instrument hold or a common airspeed for every aircraft. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-20]</a></p>
 <p><strong>Timing check:</strong> two turns × 2 min + two legs × 1 min = 6 min. Two circuits = 12 min. That arithmetic explains a pattern's duration; it does not authorise arbitrary leg extensions or leaving a clearance early. Confirm an amended plan with the controller when the assigned timing cannot be met.</p>
 <h2 id="penetration">Commence and platform</h2>
 <p>Commencing marks the transition from holding to the approach. The penetration combines descent, course management and controller handoff. Platform is an altitude checkpoint at which the published descent rate changes; it is not a DME fix. The published CV-1 profile in [1] uses 250 KIAS and 4,000 ft/min initially, reducing descent at 5,000 ft to 2,000 ft/min, then levelling at 1,200 ft. These values illustrate that source's profile, not aircraft limitations. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-23]</a></p>
 <p>Keep lateral and vertical tasks separate: descending correctly does not establish you on final bearing. When the marshal radial is offset, the source provides an intercept/arc procedure. Use the actual published approach and clearance rather than steering directly at the ship or applying a fixed deck-angle subtraction. Handoff should ideally occur before platform; check in on the assigned frequency. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-23, 2-26]</a></p>
 <h2 id="final">Final approach and landing</h2>
 <p>Arrive configured and ready for the final approach, using the aircraft's landing checks and limits. Gear, flaps, approach speed and angle of attack belong to the aircraft procedure. The Tomcat's controls and exercise gates are not module-wide rules.</p>
 <p>The source describes automatic carrier landing system (ACLS) lock-on and comparison with instrument carrier landing system (ICLS) indications before following ACLS needles. Those exchanges require that equipment and a valid lock; the words “fly needles” and “fly bullseye” are not interchangeable generic commands. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-23]</a></p>
 <p>The ball is the optical landing system's glideslope cue. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 1-10–1-13]</a> In the source's example, the controller requests the ball call at three-quarter mile. The pilot transitions from an instrument scan to outside landing cues while the landing signal officer (LSO) monitors the final landing task. Continue to monitor the approach during this transition. <a href="#source-p816">[1, p. 2-25]</a></p>
 <h2 id="radio">Communications: know what the call achieves</h2>
 <ul>
 <li><strong>Check-in and readback:</strong> establish identity and recovery information; confirm the clearance you will fly.</li>
 <li><strong>Established:</strong> report arrival in the assigned holding state.</li>
 <li><strong>Commencing:</strong> report leaving marshal to start the approach.</li>
 <li><strong>Approach check-in:</strong> establish contact after the directed handoff.</li>
 <li><strong>Platform:</strong> report the descent checkpoint.</li>
 <li><strong>Ball:</strong> report the final visual landing task, with aircraft identification and fuel state in the source example.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>These are descriptions of the exchanges in <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-26–2-27]</a>, not a universal radio script. Know your assigned channel and the meaning of the call before memorising its wording.</p>
 <h2 id="outcomes">Landing, bolter and waveoff</h2>
 <p>An arrestment ends the landing run through engagement of the arresting gear. A bolter is a touchdown without arresting-hook engagement. A waveoff discontinues the landing attempt; it can be required by the LSO or waveoff lights and is mandatory in the source procedure. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-17, A-1]</a></p>
 <p>The Case III recovery continues under instrument approach control after a bolter or waveoff. The source describes climbing, a controller-directed downwind, landing checks, an abeam fuel-state report and another turn to final. Do not import the visual Case I circuit into this sequence. Aircraft-specific climb, speed, gear/flap actions and lost-communication procedures must come from the applicable procedure; this module does not generalise the T-45C instructions to other aircraft. <a href="#source-p816">[1, pp. 2-25–2-26]</a></p>
 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>Before practising, explain these without looking back:</p>
 <details><summary>Why are BRC and final bearing different?</summary><p>BRC describes ship heading; final bearing describes the angled landing-area centreline. Use the assigned final bearing.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Does 21 DME mean you are at the marshal fix?</summary><p>No. Range alone defines a ring. You also need the assigned radial and altitude.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Is platform a distance from the ship?</summary><p>No. It is an altitude checkpoint in the descent profile.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Does a bolter mean you can join the Case I visual pattern?</summary><p>No. Follow the applicable Case III bolter procedure and controller instructions.</p></details>
 <h2 id="launch">Practice: Tomcat recovery exercise</h2>
 <section class="status-panel" data-experience-id={caseIII.id} data-experience-revision={caseIII.revision}>
 <h3>{caseIII.title}</h3><p>{caseIII.maturity} · Exercise revision {caseIII.revision}</p>
 <p>{caseIII.applicability} Prerequisites: {caseIII.prerequisites.join(' · ')}.</p>
 <p>Reinforce point-to-point navigation, marshal timing, radio handoffs and managing a descent through an approach sequence. The Tomcat is the worked example; its configuration controls and scoring do not define the general module.</p>
 <p>The exercise is self-contained: its briefing, instructor, controls and exercise notes explain how to use it without reading this page first. Read the sortie briefing and select Start Exercise. Instructor and voice are optional.</p>
 <p>It simplifies motion, wind, aircraft performance, approach guidance and arrestment; fuel consumption and bolters are not modelled. Its numeric gates and inherited dialogue remain unvalidated exercise content. It does not assess real proficiency or implement every procedure discussed above.</p>
 <p class="launch"><a class="launch-button" href={trainerHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-experience-launch data-experience-id={caseIII.id}>Launch the Tomcat exercise<span> (opens in a new window)</span></a></p>
 </section>
 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <p id="source-p816"><strong>[1] Naval Air Training Command, CNATRA P-816 (01-14), CV Procedures (UMFO), T-45C.</strong> Revision 28 January 2014, Change 1 dated 9 February 2018. <a href="https://murphysys.com/dcs/P-816.pdf">Read the Navy-authored publication (third-party mirror)</a>. Inspected 24 October 2026: optical landing system pp. 1-10–1-13; §204, pp. 2-19–2-27; weather criteria pp. 2-3–2-4; waveoff p. 2-17; glossary A-1–A-3. Historical training context, not evidence of current fleet-wide applicability. Original paraphrases and geometry only; no manual files or figures are redistributed.</p>
 <p class="note"><strong>Source conflict:</strong> this edition gives different Case III visibility thresholds in its weather criteria/glossary and recovery section. Exact weather minima are therefore unresolved here. This module teaches the night/instrument-recovery purpose without asserting a numerical threshold. It remains Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
 <p>The geometry interaction is a simplified educational model: flat station-centred coordinates, no wind, horizontal range and a fixed 250 NM/hour illustration rate. Its equations are shown with the worked example. This is not an IAS-to-groundspeed conversion or a prescribed approach.</p>
 <p>The module's source review is separate from the trainer's validation. Neither the T-45C publication nor this module validates the trainer's Tomcat gates, radio script or scoring.</p>
